# The first note in the surface's own format

*2026-11-14. A demo of the `.note` format: one file, read by Roc into the types in `format/`, drawn as a page that takes replies like any other note.*

This page was never markdown. The server found `format-demo.note` beside the markdown notes, ran the Roc program `render-note` on it, and got back the page as JSON: the title, the body, and each block a reader can reply to. Every block here takes a reply, the title and the line under it included.

**Where Roc runs.** On the server, as a program of its own. The Go server runs it once each time a `.note` file changes, the same way essay-repl-server already runs its Roc markdown renderer, and keeps the page it gets back.

## What a note is made of

A note is a title, an optional subtitle, and a list of blocks. The blocks on this page are the kinds the demo reads:

- **Paragraphs,** like this one's neighbours, with an optional bold lead.
- **Headings,** a section or a subsection.
- **Lists,** bulleted or numbered, where each point is one paragraph and may have subpoints:
  - a subpoint is one paragraph too;
  - and it holds no list of its own.
- **Quotes, code, tables and diagrams,** each below.
- **A divider,** at the end.

A link goes to another note by its name, like [the notes](arrays-that-are-values), or to the web, like [Roc's site](https://www.roc-lang.org/). Bold holds `code` but never *italics*, and that rule is in the types, not in a check.

### Numbered, from where it left off

5. A numbered list keeps the number it starts at.
6. So a count that goes on past a heading stays right.

> **The rule for a quote.** Each of its paragraphs takes a reply of its own.
>
> This is the second one.

## The code

The parser's entry, as it stands in `format/Parse.roc`:

```roc
note : Str -> Try(Note, Parse.Problem)
```

## The table

Where a reply attaches, for each kind of block:

| Block | A reply attaches to |
|---|---|
| A paragraph or a heading | the block |
| A list | each point, and each subpoint |
| A quote | each of its paragraphs |
| A table | the whole table, as in a markdown note |

## The diagram

How a `.note` becomes the page you are reading:

```dot
digraph note_to_page {
  rankdir=LR;
  node [shape=box, style=rounded];
  file [label="format-demo.note"];
  go [label="Go server\nstore.go"];
  roc [label="render-note\n(Roc)"];
  page [label="the page\nwith replies"];
  file -> go [label="changed"];
  go -> roc [label="the text,\nas arguments"];
  roc -> go [label="JSON"];
  go -> page;
}
```

---

The viz.js in the page draws the diagram, the same library and the same defaults essay-repl-server uses for a `dot` fence.
